  <div class="ad2e-manual-body">

  {{#if is.start}}
  <h2>Welcome</h2>
  <p>This guide explains how to use the AD&amp;D 2nd Edition character sheet in Foundry: creating a character, buying
  proficiencies, carrying equipment, casting spells, fighting and riding. The rules themselves are in your books; item
  sheets link to the matching page of the AD&amp;D 2e wiki (the book icon).</p>
  <p>Every window and roll dialog can be resized by dragging its bottom-right corner; when a dialog is taller than the
  screen, scroll inside it to reach its buttons.</p>
  <h3>The character sheet</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Main</strong>: ability scores, saving throws, hit points (Damage, Heal, Rest, Bind wounds), experience
    and Level Up.</li>
    <li><strong>Combat</strong>: hit points, THAC0, movement, the weapons in hand with Attack and Damage, armour and
    Armour Class, and your class abilities.</li>
    <li><strong>Equipment</strong>: everything you own: weapons, armour, gear, coins, containers, magical items, and the
    animals you own or ride.</li>
    <li><strong>Race &amp; Class</strong>: race, class and kit, with their requirements, level limits and the kit's weapon limits (a weapon outside your class or kit list is marked on the weapon row and in the attack dialog).</li>
    <li><strong>Class Abilities</strong>: thief, bard and ranger skills, backstab, turning undead, paladin powers.</li>
    <li><strong>Proficiencies</strong>: weapon and nonweapon proficiencies and their checks; languages; traits and disadvantages.</li>
    <li><strong>Spells</strong>: your spells by level, memorization and casting (only shown if your character has spells).</li>
    <li><strong>Abilities</strong>: the detailed ability tables, ability checks and tests such as bend bars.</li>
    <li><strong>Biography</strong>: notes, age, gender, height and weight, henchmen, followers and stronghold, familiar, and an Animal Master's or Rider's bonded animal.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Most things are added by dragging them from a compendium (the Compendium sidebar tab) onto the sheet. Every roll
  opens a dialog with a modifier and a reason for anything special your GM allows.</p>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.create}}
  <h2>Creating a character</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Create the actor</strong>: Actors sidebar &gt; Create Actor &gt; type Character (or your GM makes one for you).</li>
    <li><strong>Ability scores</strong>: type the rolled scores on the Main tab, or click the dice next to "Abilities"
    to roll with the method your GM chooses (Methods II-VI). Warriors with Strength 18 can roll exceptional Strength.</li>
    <li><strong>Race</strong>: drag a race from "Races (PHB)". Its adjustments are added to your scores; the sheet warns
    when a score is outside the race's limits.</li>
    <li><strong>Age</strong>: on the Bio tab, "Roll" next to Age rolls your race's starting age. As your character grows
    older, the middle age, old and venerable changes to the ability scores are applied for you (shown next to the
    age).</li>
    <li><strong>Languages</strong>: on the Proficiencies tab. Your native language (empty = your race's own tongue) and
    the world's common language are free. Add further languages by typing a name or picking one (your race's initial
    languages come first): modern languages count against Intelligence Table 4 or your Languages, Modern slots (your GM
    decides which), ancient ones against Languages, Ancient slots. Tick "Reads/writes" for each language you can read;
    those count against Reading/Writing slots. If your GM has languages learned in play, only the GM adds them.</li>
    <li><strong>Class</strong>: drag a class from "Classes (PHB)". The Race &amp; Class tab shows whether your scores meet
    its minimums, the alignments allowed, your prime requisite bonus and your race's level limit.</li>
    <li><strong>Kit</strong> (optional, if your GM uses kits): drag a kit from "Class Kits". A kit that does not suit your
    class or race is refused or marked. Its bonus proficiencies are added for free (you may be asked to choose one);
    its bonuses and penalties are applied for you, or offered as tick boxes in the roll dialogs when they depend on the
    situation.</li>
    <li><strong>Alignment, name and picture</strong>: set them in the header; click the picture to change it.</li>
    <li><strong>Hit points</strong>: "Roll 1st-Level HP" on the Main tab.</li>
    <li><strong>Proficiencies, equipment and spells</strong>: see the following sections.</li>
    <li><strong>Gender, height and weight</strong>: on the Bio tab pick a gender (male, female, non-binary or agender) and,
    "Roll (Table 10)" there rolls height and body weight: it always asks whether to use the male or female base figures
    of PHB Table 10, whatever the gender, and records the choice (the "Height &amp; weight" column, used as the default next time). Body weight is needed when
    riding; it can also be entered or rolled on the Equipment tab.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Each character has at most one kit.</p>
  <h3>Skills &amp; Powers kits</h3>
  <p>Roll your social rank on the Race &amp; Class tab. Weapon Masters specialize (or take expertise) in a melee weapon,
  which becomes their chosen weapon on the Proficiencies tab (proficiencies of another weapon type are marked), and use
  "Display" on the Combat tab when a fight starts; Mystics "Meditate" on the Race &amp; Class tab (the boost starts when
  the meditation ends). Pugilists and Barbarians are handled in your GM's reaction rolls and in unarmed attacks.</p>
  <h3>Multi-classing</h3>
  <p>If your GM allows multi-class characters, a dwarf, elf, gnome, halfling or half-elf can have two or three classes in
  the combinations the race allows. Drag the first class, then drag the second onto the sheet and choose "Multi-class"
  (the dialog lists your race's combinations). Do this before play: all classes start at 1st level. Then roll 1st-level
  hit points. The Race &amp; Class tab lists your classes with their levels and experience; experience you earn is divided
  between them, and each class has its own Level Up button. You use the best THAC0 and saving throws of your classes and
  all their abilities and spells. A kit must suit one of your classes; a bard combination (Complete Bard's Handbook) needs
  one of the bard kits the Race &amp; Class tab lists. A multi-class priest keeps the priest's weapon limits, and casting
  wizard spells in armour asks first (an elf in elven chain may cast freely).</p>
  <h3>Dual-classing</h3>
  <p>If your GM allows dual-class characters, a human with high enough scores can change class: drag the new class onto
  the sheet and choose "Dual-class". You start the new class at 1st level and keep your hit points; the old class's
  abilities and spells stay on the sheet. Until your new level is higher than the old one, using them (or ticking "Use the
  earlier class's number" in an attack or save dialog) costs you experience for that encounter and adventure, and Level Up
  gives no hit points. The Race &amp; Class tab shows where you stand. A switch made by mistake can be undone by your GM.</p>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.proficiencies}}
  <h2>Proficiencies</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Drag weapon and nonweapon proficiencies from the "Proficiencies" compendium onto the sheet. The Proficiencies tab
    shows the slots used and available; proficiencies outside your class's groups cost one slot more, and the count
    turns red if you spend too many.</li>
    <li><strong>Nonweapon checks</strong>: click the "≤ number" button to roll a d20 against it. To spend extra slots on a
    proficiency (+1 each), open it and set "Extra slots".</li>
    <li><strong>Weapon proficiencies</strong> remove the non-proficiency penalty for that weapon. Fighters may tick
    "Specialized" on one weapon (it costs extra slots).</li>
    <li><strong>Skills &amp; Powers weapon rules</strong> (if your GM uses them): weapon groups, fighting styles, armour
    and shield proficiencies, weapon of choice (+1 to hit), expertise and mastery are tick boxes and items on this tab;
    their character-point costs are paid in weapon slots, shown on each row.</li>
    <li><strong>Traits and disadvantages</strong> (if your GM allows them): drag them from "Traits &amp; Disadvantages".
    The traits you take must be paid for with the points of your disadvantages; the tab shows the balance.</li>
  </ul>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.equipment}}
  <h2>Equipment, money and containers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Buying gear</strong>: drag weapons, armour and equipment from "Weapons (PHB)", "Armour (PHB)" and
    "Equipment (PHB)" onto the sheet. Change a quantity with the -/+ buttons. Coins are items too: drag the coin from
    "Equipment (PHB)" and set the amount.</li>
    <li><strong>Class weapons</strong>: a weapon your class may not use is marked in red; you can still attack with it if
    your GM allows.</li>
    <li><strong>Equipping</strong>: tick "Equipped" on a weapon to have it in hand, and on armour or a shield to wear
    it; your Armour Class and attacks follow. On the Combat tab, "Stow" puts a weapon away and "Drop" drops it
    ("Pick up" on the Equipment tab).</li>
    <li><strong>Carried</strong>: untick "Carried" for things you leave behind (at the inn, on the horse); they stop
    counting toward your encumbrance. The Equipment tab shows your load and movement.</li>
    <li><strong>Waterskins</strong>: the "Full"/"Empty" button on a waterskin's (or wineskin's) row fills or empties it;
    full, it weighs 9 lb instead of 1 lb for encumbrance.</li>
    <li><strong>Containers</strong>: backpacks, sacks, pouches and chests hold other items. Drag an item's row onto the
    container in the Containers list, or choose "In container" on the item's sheet; "Take out" removes it. Each
    container shows its load against its capacity.</li>
    <li><strong>Ammunition</strong>: arrows, bolts and sling stones are items; shooting asks which ammunition you use and
    spends one.</li>
    <li><strong>Magical items</strong>: "Use" spends a charge, a potion or a scroll and posts it to chat. Items you find
    may be unidentified until your GM identifies them; their bonuses still work.</li>
  </ul>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.spells}}
  <h2>Spells</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Adding spells</strong>: drag a spell from "Spells (examples)" or from your GM's "Imported Spells"
    compendium onto the sheet. Wizards and bards are asked to roll to learn it; a spell you fail to learn stays listed
    with a Learn button for when you may try again.</li>
    <li><strong>Memorizing</strong>: on the Spells tab, use -/+ to memorize spells in your slots for each spell level.</li>
    <li><strong>Casting</strong>: "Cast" posts the spell to chat and uses one memorized casting. Spells that deal damage
    or heal have a Damage button that rolls the dice listed for the spell (you choose the option if there are several).</li>
    <li><strong>Targets and saves</strong>: target the creatures before you click Cast. Creatures with magic resistance
    roll it at once (a resisted spell has no effect on them). If the spell allows a saving throw, the chat card gets a
    Save button for each remaining target; your Damage roll then gives full damage to those who failed (or have not
    rolled yet) and half to those who saved, or nothing for a "Neg." spell. Your own magic resistance (from an item)
    goes next to your saving throws on the Main tab; tick Lowered to let a friendly spell through.</li>
    <li><strong>Resting</strong>: "Rest" restores your memorized spells after a night's rest.</li>
    <li><strong>Components</strong>: if your GM tracks material components, casting uses up the ones the spell consumes
    from your items (you are warned when one is missing).</li>
  </ul>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.combat}}
  <h2>Combat</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Targets</strong>: target the enemy's token first (right-click it &gt; target, or the T key). The attack
    dialog then knows its Armour Class, and the GM can apply your damage to it from the chat message.</li>
    <li><strong>Attacking</strong>: on the Combat tab, click a weapon's Attack button. The dialog offers range, two
    weapons, mounted combat and the situational modifiers; with automatic damage on, a hit rolls the damage too,
    otherwise click Damage.</li>
    <li><strong>Unarmed</strong>: Punch, Wrestle and Overbear are in the weapon list (Combat and Equipment tabs).</li>
    <li><strong>Traps</strong> (thieves): target the trapped object or trap before Find/Remove Traps; the roll then offers
    the trap's difficulty and a silent attempt (-10%, a click on 01-10). A roll of 96-100 springs the trap.</li>
    <li><strong>Lasso and net</strong>: attacking with either opens its own dialog (lasso: legs, arms, unhorse, or
    spurring your mount; net: throw, loop the rope, pull/trip, fold); target the defender first. Caught in a net, use
    "Break free (net)" on the Combat tab.</li>
    <li><strong>Maneuvers</strong> (if your GM uses them): the attack dialog's Maneuver select offers a called shot, sap,
    disarm, grab, trap, block, pull/trip and, with a shield, shield-punch and shield-rush. Opposed maneuvers ask for the
    defender's numbers (already filled in from your target) and roll both sides.</li>
    <li><strong>Critical hits</strong> (if your GM uses them): a natural 18 or more that hits by 5 or more doubles the
    damage dice (rolled for you); with the detailed system the chat shows where the blow landed and asks the victim for a
    save vs. death.</li>
    <li><strong>Poison</strong>: when a poisoned attack hits you, chat shows a save request with a Save button next to
    your character (dwarves and halflings get their bonus vs. poison automatically). When the poison takes effect is
    the GM's secret; the Main tab shows that you are poisoned, paralyzed or debilitated. To poison your own weapon or
    arrows, own a poison item and use "Coat with poison" on the weapon's or ammunition's sheet; each damage roll uses a
    dose.</li>
    <li><strong>Item saves</strong>: fire, falls and the like can destroy what you carry. Each item sheet has a Material
    (guessed from the item; change it if the guess is wrong). A destroyed item is marked "(destroyed)" and no longer
    carried or equipped.</li>
    <li><strong>Initiative</strong>: when the GM starts combat, roll initiative from the combat tracker; you may be asked
    what you are doing this round (weapon, spell, item).</li>
    <li><strong>Saving throws and checks</strong>: saving throws on the Main tab; ability checks and tests on the
    Abilities tab; jumping from the jump distances at the bottom of the sheet; thief skills and turning undead on the Class Abilities tab.</li>
    <li><strong>Surprise</strong>: the Surprise button on the Main or Combat tab, when your GM asks for it.</li>
    <li><strong>Hit points</strong>: Damage and Heal on the Main tab; "Bind wounds" after a fight; "Rest" for natural
    healing over days.</li>
  </ul>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.riding}}
  <h2>Mounts, pack animals and riding</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Get the animal as an actor you own (your GM can give you one from "Hirelings &amp; Mounts").</li>
    <li>Drag the animal's actor from the Actors sidebar onto your character sheet. It appears on the Equipment tab with
    its load and movement.</li>
    <li>Tick <strong>Riding</strong> next to the animal you ride (one at a time). Its load now includes you: your body
    weight plus everything you carry, so set your body weight first.</li>
    <li>Gear you put on the animal (saddle bags, packs) goes on the animal's own sheet, in its Inventory.</li>
    <li>On the map, put your token next to your mount's token: when either moves, the other moves with it (if your GM
    has this setting on).</li>
    <li>Fighting from horseback: the attack dialog offers the mounted options (untrained mount, attacking a smaller
    creature, shooting from a moving mount).</li>
    <li>Pushing a mount beyond its normal pace is the "Push" button on the mount's sheet.</li>
    <li><strong>Animal Master and Rider kits</strong>: your bonded companion or mount is on the Biography tab. Drag its
    actor onto your sheet and accept the bond; a Rider sees the mount's health and, when both tokens are on the map,
    how far away it is and in which direction. A mount too big or too small for your race, or a companion that does not
    suit your race or alignment, is marked for your GM to decide. If your mount dies through your negligence and you fail
    the saving throw, you are feebleminded for some hours (shown on the Biography tab; casting asks first). Cavaliers and
    Nobles must own a mount (the Race &amp; Class tab reminds you).</li>
  </ol>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.advancing}}
  <h2>Experience and advancing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Your GM awards experience; they are in the header (the + adds an award); hover over the field to see the amount needed for the next level.</li>
    <li>When you have enough, click <strong>Level Up</strong> on the Main tab: it rolls the new hit points. Saving
    throws, THAC0, proficiency slots, spell slots and class abilities follow the new level.</li>
    <li>Thieves and bards spend their new skill points on the Class Abilities tab.</li>
    <li>Multi-class characters advance each class separately: use the class's Level Up button on the Race &amp; Class tab.</li>
    <li>Levels lost to energy drain (your GM applies them) are listed on the Main tab until you regain them with Level Up
    or a restoration spell (which ages you two years). Memorized spells beyond your new slots are forgotten: your GM
    chooses which with you.</li>
    <li>New proficiency slots can be filled from the "Proficiencies" compendium.</li>
  </ul>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.more}}
  <h2>Henchmen, familiars and more</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Henchmen</strong>: drag a character or NPC actor onto your sheet; it is listed on the Biography tab with
    its morale and loyalty. (Animals dragged onto the sheet go to the Equipment tab instead.)</li>
    <li><strong>Travel</strong>: the movement line at the bottom of your sheet shows your daily march and force march
    in miles; the info icon lists your rate in difficult terrain for one round. After force marching, the days of
    penalty show there in red (your GM's travel card rolls the checks; resting removes them).</li>
    <li><strong>Followers</strong>: the Biography tab records your stronghold; when your class reaches its level (and the
    stronghold or place of worship is marked built where needed), your GM rolls your followers once and they appear there. "Construction" there plans a castle (site, modules, workers,
    helpers) with its cost and building time; your GM starts the work and it progresses as game time passes.</li>
    <li><strong>Familiars</strong> (wizards): "Find Familiar" on the Biography tab; your GM rolls the result.</li>
    <li><strong>Sha'ir</strong> (Al-Qadim): your gen is summoned from the sheet and fetches your spells.</li>
    <li><strong>Notes</strong>: write anything else on the Biography tab.</li>
    <li>Ask your GM for the "AD&amp;D 2e GM manual", which lists the world's settings (death rule, initiative method,
    encumbrance and optional rules).</li>
  </ul>
  {{/if}}
  </div>
</section>
